WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.257
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:09.380
- Well, welcome back to another
episode of the "Addy Hour."

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:11.550
I'm honored today to be
able to host a conversation

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:14.020
about oxytocin and social behavior,

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:15.600
community and mental health.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.700
In a lot of ways, these
topics have come up

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.640
in a lot of our conversations
on this podcast already,

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.114
but we really haven't been
able to dig into the science

00:00:23.114 --> 00:00:25.080
of social behavior and community.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.160
So today I'm honored

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.700
to be able to host two revered scientists,

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.610
a revered scientist and philosopher,

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:33.880
and luminaries in this area

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.380
to really engage in this conversation.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:39.543
So the first guest I'd like to
introduce is Dr. Larry Young.

00:00:39.543 --> 00:00:41.040
Larry is a director

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.380
of the Center for Translational
Social Neuroscience

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.870
and the director of the Silvio O. Conte

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:48.050
Center for Oxytocin and Social Cognition

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:49.780
at Emory University in Atlanta.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:52.510
He's also the William P.
Timmie Professor of Psychiatry

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:54.260
at the Emory School of Medicine.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:57.980
And he leads the Center
for Social Neural Networks

00:00:57.980 --> 00:00:59.870
at the University of Tsukuba in Japan.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.150
So quite a few different leadership roles.

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:05.100
He's someone who's also been a
recipient of numerous awards,

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.560
including the Golden Brain
Award, the Frank Beach Award,

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.390
the ACNP Daniel H. Efron Award,

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:11.280
and he's a fellow

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.890
in the American Academy
of Arts and Sciences.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:16.790
His research focuses on the
neurobiological mechanisms

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:18.490
underlying social relationships,

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:20.180
and how we can translate some of that

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.795
into treatments for psychiatric illnesses

00:01:22.795 --> 00:01:25.460
with social deficits, such as autism.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:27.640
He's someone that I had the
pleasure of interacting with

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.830
over the years as a fellow neuroscientist.

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:31.760
He's actually hosted me at his institution

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:33.880
and served in some
mentoring roles as well,

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:35.210
which I'm really grateful for.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.400
So today I'm honored to be
able to welcome him here

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:38.720
to the "Addy Hour" podcast.

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:39.815
Thanks for being here, Larry.

00:01:39.815 --> 00:01:41.024
- Thank you for inviting me.

00:01:41.024 --> 00:01:43.640
It's wonderful, thank you.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:45.560
- The second guest which
I'd like to introduce

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:48.120
is Dr. Patricia Smith Churchland,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.020
who is a professor emerita of philosophy

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.900
at the University of California San Diego

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.520
and also an adjunct professor
at the Salk Institute.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:57.980
She is someone who has been a pioneer

00:01:57.980 --> 00:01:59.350
in several different ways,

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:00.780
specifically, her work focuses

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.320
on discoveries in neuroscience

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:05.500
and how that impacts
traditional philosophical ideas.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:06.925
She's the author of several books,

00:02:06.925 --> 00:02:09.120
one, the pioneer book, "Neurophilosophy,"

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.809
but also the book "The
Computational Brain,"

00:02:11.809 --> 00:02:13.290
also "Braintrust:

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:15.717
What Neuroscience Tells
Us about Morality,"

00:02:15.717 --> 00:02:17.357
"Touching A Nerve,"

00:02:17.357 --> 00:02:20.150
"Conscience: The Origins
of Moral Intuition."

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:24.210
And she has been just a celebrated speaker

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:25.260
over the years as well,

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:27.360
having had listened to some of her recent

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:28.910
and talks from a few years back,

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:30.630
I've been really impressed with the way

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:33.220
that she really has brought
this neurophilosophy

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:34.450
to the forefront.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:35.920
She's someone who's
been the past president

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.090
of the American Philosophical Association

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:40.550
and The Society for
Philosophy and Psychology.

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:43.300
She won a MacArthur Prize in 1991,

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:45.550
a Rossi Prize for Neuroscience in 2008,

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:48.890
and a PROSE Prize for Science
for her book "Braintrust."

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:51.330
She's someone, again, as I
mentioned, who's very celebrated,

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:54.790
who was also a previous
philosophy department chair

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.240
at the University of California San Diego.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.680
So I'm honored to be able
to welcome Pat Churchland

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:01.130
to the "Addy Hour" podcast as well.

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:02.550
Thanks for being here.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:04.050
- Thanks so much for having me.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.500
It's really a great pleasure.

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:08.000
- Well, I'm grateful to both of you

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:09.110
for the work that you do.

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:10.920
And again, this I think is a conversation

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:13.645
that's been a long time
coming on this podcast

00:03:13.645 --> 00:03:14.860
as these topics

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.020
and your names have actually
come up on previous episodes,

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:19.794
so full circle for us in a lot of ways.

00:03:19.794 --> 00:03:21.400
But as my listeners know,

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.190
I often just like to
start out and check in

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:24.590
with how people are doing.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:26.870
Both of you as we can hear from your CVs,

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:28.630
have very active lives.

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:29.640
And so I'm curious to know

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.480
how you're both doing in this moment

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:35.850
and also what occupies you and
your time on a daily basis?

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:37.460
So Pat, actually, if we
could start with you,

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:38.683
that'd be great.

00:03:38.683 --> 00:03:43.000
- Well, I'm doing very well
actually, but I'm retired.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:48.000
And so I can do what I want with my days.

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:53.850
And I find that to be an
absolutely amazing experience.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:57.460
So I do a lot of reading, of course,

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.860
but I also walk the dog a lot,

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:03.200
and I do about an hour of yoga every day

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.540
and meet up with my friends
for drinks in the afternoon

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:08.457
and like that.

00:04:08.457 --> 00:04:09.923
I also watch some football.

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:13.058
- Wow, so a full plate.

00:04:13.058 --> 00:04:15.500
I mean, that's encouraging
to hear how active you are.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:16.333
You said you're retired,

00:04:16.333 --> 00:04:18.240
but you sound still very
active in a lot of ways,

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:19.740
which is excellent.
- Lots of ways, yeah.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:21.700
Well, at a certain point,

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:24.800
of course, your body will
tell you you're done.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:26.180
And it hasn't told me that yet,

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:29.100
so I'm still going.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.530
- Excellent, excellent.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:32.400
Larry, what about you?

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:34.170
- Yeah, things are going
real well with us here.

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:37.420
We're able to work in the lab

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:40.220
and come to work every day and see people.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:42.517
There was a time when we
didn't get to do that,

00:04:42.517 --> 00:04:45.300
and that really was strange.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.490
And I didn't like that very much,

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:51.250
but so far we've been able
to adjust pretty well.

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:52.083
During the pandemic,

00:04:52.083 --> 00:04:55.090
I decided to really try to get healthy,

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:56.560
and I started exercising.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:58.928
So now every day I sort
of start my day off

00:04:58.928 --> 00:05:02.910
with an exercise routine
and come into work

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:07.390
and just try to get as much
social interaction as I can.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:08.720
One thing I do miss though

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:11.740
is being able to travel as
much as I used to be able to,

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:15.860
really meeting other
scientists face to face.

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:17.040
That's so important.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.762
And we've just been missing
that for a long time now.

00:05:19.762 --> 00:05:21.390
- Yeah, yeah.

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:22.795
- Yeah, I find that too.

00:05:22.795 --> 00:05:26.610
I mean, I find that Zoom works quite well

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:29.900
when you're talking to
people you already know.

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:33.960
But if like I'm going
to be giving a Zoom talk

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.120
to folks in Saudi Arabia,

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.960
and I would so much rather be there

00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:43.340
and then have a chance
to interact with them

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:46.930
and talk about the work and so forth.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:48.938
And so that is disappointing,

00:05:48.938 --> 00:05:51.230
but really in the long haul of life,

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:53.253
it's not something I can complain about.

00:05:53.253 --> 00:05:54.400
- Right, right.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:56.370
But just that opportunity
that we had before to,

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:58.350
I mean, in a sense, experience the culture

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:00.752
in a different way than you would-

00:06:00.752 --> 00:06:01.585
- Absolutely.
- On the computer.

00:06:01.585 --> 00:06:03.620
Yeah, yeah, so that makes a lot of sense.

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:04.453
I know we're gonna get

00:06:04.453 --> 00:06:06.040
to the social behavior piece eventually,

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:08.390
but it's just, as I'm
listening to both of you talk

00:06:08.390 --> 00:06:09.751
and thinking about your areas of research,

00:06:09.751 --> 00:06:11.930
and as you're sharing about the losses

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:13.160
of having those connections,

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:16.280
I think a lot of things are
full circle in some ways,

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:18.870
although I will start with
a question, in a sense,

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:19.703
for both of you,

00:06:19.703 --> 00:06:23.690
do you think, even as people
who study social behavior,

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:25.530
did it catch you off guard at all

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:28.040
at how much we've missed those rhythms

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:28.900
or how it's affected us,

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:31.200
or would you say that
was completely expected?

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:33.643
- Well, I don't know.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:37.906
I think people do vary,

00:06:37.906 --> 00:06:42.380
both as a function of temperament
and as a function of age

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:46.210
and what kinds of routines and skills

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:48.410
and so forth they have

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:52.860
and how much the isolation
has affected them.

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:56.780
It affects everybody to
some degree of course,

00:06:56.780 --> 00:07:01.191
but it's clear that some people

00:07:01.191 --> 00:07:05.100
are much more deeply
bothered by it than others.

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:10.030
And I'm not quite sure
scientifically what that tells us.

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:11.560
- Right, right.

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:12.393
- Yeah. I agree.

00:07:12.393 --> 00:07:14.470
I think that there are some people who,

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:17.540
well, maybe at first they
feel they're thriving

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:20.160
by being at home and
maybe they're introverted.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:22.570
They don't, somehow social interactions

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:26.963
are a little bit, give them anxiety.

00:07:26.963 --> 00:07:28.990
But then I think that

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:31.070
by completely avoiding
those social interactions,

00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:33.393
but even when we have the opportunity now,

00:07:34.557 --> 00:07:37.205
I think that's really
doing maybe some damage

00:07:37.205 --> 00:07:39.445
to their mental health,

00:07:39.445 --> 00:07:41.590
and especially if they continue

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:43.745
to avoid the social interactions

00:07:43.745 --> 00:07:45.500
because they really are healthy.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:46.830
- Yeah.

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:48.300
- Although it's funny,

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:51.140
I was reflecting on all of this

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:52.225
and thinking about

00:07:52.225 --> 00:07:56.480
I grew up in this very remote
area of British Columbia

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:59.917
and there were lots of people

00:07:59.917 --> 00:08:03.480
who would be off
prospecting in the mountains

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:07.740
for months and months on
end, maybe not even a dog.

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:10.050
And then there were the fur trappers

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:11.729
a little bit further North

00:08:11.729 --> 00:08:16.550
who would be out in the
Bush all winter trapping.

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:19.950
And then they would come in
in the spring with their furs

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:21.650
and they poop it up a little bit.

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:22.534
And back they though.

00:08:22.534 --> 00:08:26.440
And it made me wonder,

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:31.440
is it perhaps that the
expectation of sociality

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:34.900
and then not getting

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:37.140
that we find particularly painful

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:40.183
because I mean these old
prospectors and fur trappers,

00:08:40.183 --> 00:08:43.230
well, their social skills were terrific.

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:44.683
As you may imagine then of course,

00:08:44.683 --> 00:08:46.773
they certainly needed a bath.

00:08:49.483 --> 00:08:51.500
But on the other hand,

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:55.900
they did not seem to
have suffered mentally

00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:59.170
as a result of being on
their own for so long.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:02.289
And I mean, it was just part of life then.

00:09:02.289 --> 00:09:03.756
But now I'm back on,

00:09:03.756 --> 00:09:05.756
and I think, how did those guys do that?

00:09:07.107 --> 00:09:07.940
- Yeah, well, yeah.

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:09.600
I think certainly there's
individual variation

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:14.040
in how much we need social interactions.

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:16.570
But I think for young people
it's especially important

00:09:16.570 --> 00:09:18.235
because there's still developing.

00:09:18.235 --> 00:09:21.660
I mean, students and graduate students

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:24.060
and people that are early
phases of their career,

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:27.180
I think that social
interactions is so important

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:29.840
for building the networks
that help them succeed.

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:32.290
Those trappers maybe they were
already fixed in their ways

00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:34.023
and all they needed to do is trap.

00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:35.923
- Probably were.

00:09:35.923 --> 00:09:38.270
- So but they're building
that social skills

00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:40.600
because things are going to change again

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.000
in two, three, four years.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:47.990
Our livelihood is going to
require social interactions

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:49.510
and social skills.

00:09:49.510 --> 00:09:50.422
I'm just afraid that some people

00:09:50.422 --> 00:09:54.210
they're missing element
on the normal development

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:55.043
of those skills.

00:09:55.043 --> 00:09:55.904
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:09:55.904 --> 00:09:58.240
- Yeah. I think those are
really important points.

00:09:58.240 --> 00:09:59.073
I'm intrigued about that.

00:09:59.073 --> 00:10:00.670
Not that there's a clear answer,

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:02.577
but the expectation component too,

00:10:02.577 --> 00:10:04.570
and how that plays into it.

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:07.730
So I mean, you could just hear
that in the public discourse

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:10.000
and how much angst there seemed to be

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:12.380
around the expectation to be together

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:14.407
and the inability to be able to do that,

00:10:14.407 --> 00:10:17.030
and to have to facilitate
it through other means.

00:10:17.030 --> 00:10:17.863
And then Larry to your point,

00:10:17.863 --> 00:10:19.440
I mean, all the developmental components,

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:21.840
I mean, there's obviously an
intersection there as well.

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:23.490
I know that that's something
that people will try

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:25.090
and look at down the road

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:29.010
is definitely a question that
will have long-term impacts.

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:31.080
I mean, just developmentally
thinking about how,

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:33.890
I mean, even if we think
about younger kids,

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:35.530
preschool and elementary school,

00:10:35.530 --> 00:10:37.470
what impact this is going
to have down the road

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:38.600
for their social development.

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:40.506
- Even those kids they're going to school

00:10:40.506 --> 00:10:43.090
and they've went for a couple of years

00:10:43.090 --> 00:10:45.990
not seeing the faces of other kids

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:47.590
or their facial expression.

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:50.750
We learn from those real
social interactions,

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:55.470
those rewiring that
happens to help us learn

00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:57.370
what other people are
thinking, reading emotions,

00:10:57.370 --> 00:10:58.203
and they're missing that.

00:10:58.203 --> 00:11:00.180
And I think it's really kind of opened up

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:04.033
a whole new field of study
for the next decade or so.

00:11:05.117 --> 00:11:07.340
What has been the impact of this.

00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:10.339
The other thing that's
changing things so much

00:11:10.339 --> 00:11:12.610
COVID came, this Zoom

00:11:12.610 --> 00:11:16.880
and the ability to interact
so easily over Zoom.

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:19.480
And that's sort of really taking the place

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:22.120
of a lot of regular social interactions

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:24.387
and what are the consequences of that,

00:11:24.387 --> 00:11:26.340
and also dating.

00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:28.400
People have had a hard time dating.

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:29.570
It's really and now...

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:31.730
So for a couple of years,

00:11:31.730 --> 00:11:34.220
people will have to
date in a different way.

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:37.533
And so that can have a long
lasting impact as well.

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:41.420
- So much to process and think about,

00:11:41.420 --> 00:11:42.660
I definitely want to come back to that,

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:45.200
but even as we've already
touched on so many topics

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:47.407
about development and the
social interactions as well.

00:11:47.407 --> 00:11:49.050
I want to just take a step back

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:50.840
and have you all actually take us through

00:11:50.840 --> 00:11:51.916
the science and philosophy

00:11:51.916 --> 00:11:54.120
of the social bio and
social relationships.

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:55.890
'Cause I know so many of our audience

00:11:55.890 --> 00:11:57.370
are familiar with that.

00:11:57.370 --> 00:11:59.010
Some are vaguely familiar with it,

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:01.040
have heard about oxytocin a little bit,

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:03.030
or I've heard about the love hormone

00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:08.030
as people sometimes make it
out to be over simplistic.

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:10.280
But in a sense, just want
to go and start there

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:12.640
and have you share what we know

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:14.670
both from your own research
and your own thinking

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:15.800
and from the field as well.

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:16.633
So Larry, can you take us through

00:12:16.633 --> 00:12:18.281
a little bit of the biology

00:12:18.281 --> 00:12:20.490
of what we know at this point?

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:23.170
- Yeah. Well, let me first
say something about oxytocin

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:24.061
since you mentioned that,

00:12:24.061 --> 00:12:26.890
'cause this is a really important molecule

00:12:26.890 --> 00:12:27.940
for social interactions,

00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:31.380
and it was first discovered
for its role in giving birth.

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:33.950
It's the molecule that's
released from the mother's brain

00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:35.020
when it's time to give birth

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:36.723
and it causes uterus contractions.

00:12:36.723 --> 00:12:38.870
And then after the baby is born,

00:12:38.870 --> 00:12:41.530
the baby begins to circle, to nurse.

00:12:41.530 --> 00:12:44.410
The oxytocin is released to eject the milk

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:48.990
so the baby can get the milk to thrive.

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:51.050
But what we also know is that

00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:52.530
it's also acting in the brain

00:12:52.530 --> 00:12:55.583
to focus the attention
of the mother to the baby

00:12:55.583 --> 00:12:57.347
and to bond with that baby

00:12:57.347 --> 00:13:00.100
and to feel a real sense of connection.

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:03.190
And that was some of the first knowledge

00:13:03.190 --> 00:13:05.190
that we knew about oxytocin
and social behavior.

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:09.010
And then in my work, we started
working with these voles.

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:11.264
These are little rodents,
they look like hamsters,

00:13:11.264 --> 00:13:12.730
they're natural animals

00:13:12.730 --> 00:13:14.400
that live out in the
Midwest United States.

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:17.290
And there were two
different kinds of voles.

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:18.930
There was one called the Prairie vole,

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:22.250
which is, they have a family life,

00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:24.620
very similar to our own.

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:27.010
Male and female come together, they mate.

00:13:27.010 --> 00:13:29.470
After mating, a transformation
happens in the brain

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:30.771
and they form a bond

00:13:30.771 --> 00:13:33.970
and then they basically
live in that partnership

00:13:33.970 --> 00:13:35.020
for the rest of their life.

00:13:35.020 --> 00:13:37.210
They raise their offspring together.

00:13:37.210 --> 00:13:40.350
Then the other species is a meadow vole,

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:42.710
they're solitary, they're
like those trappers.

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:44.463
They prefer to be by themselves.

00:13:45.610 --> 00:13:47.050
So we've been studying them to see

00:13:47.050 --> 00:13:49.670
what is the chemistry of that
bond in the prairie voles.

00:13:49.670 --> 00:13:52.010
And it turns out that oxytocin,

00:13:52.010 --> 00:13:54.560
the same molecule that's
responsible for giving birth

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:57.340
is also helping to seal that bond

00:13:57.340 --> 00:13:58.750
between these prairie voles

00:13:58.750 --> 00:14:01.031
that bond that lasts a lifetime.

00:14:01.031 --> 00:14:05.380
And oxytocin interacts with
other molecules like dopamine,

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:08.140
which is involved in reward and pleasure

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:10.200
in the opiate system.

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:11.460
And what we've kind of learned

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:14.938
is that a pair bond, at least
the strong romantic bond

00:14:14.938 --> 00:14:19.653
is very much like an
addiction to a partner.

00:14:20.597 --> 00:14:22.830
But now we know oxytocin

00:14:22.830 --> 00:14:27.540
is also really important for
many other kinds of bonds.

00:14:27.540 --> 00:14:30.944
Bonds between a dog and the owner.

00:14:30.944 --> 00:14:33.780
When your dog looks at you in the eyes,

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:36.364
it causes you to release oxytocin

00:14:36.364 --> 00:14:38.264
and then you want to nurture that dog.

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:43.430
Also just social touch,
looking eye to eye contact

00:14:43.430 --> 00:14:46.190
causes oxytocin release.

00:14:46.190 --> 00:14:49.400
And it seems that what
oxytocin is really doing

00:14:49.400 --> 00:14:53.759
is helping tune the brain
into the social stimuli

00:14:53.759 --> 00:14:56.560
so that we pay attention to that

00:14:56.560 --> 00:15:00.713
and can navigate our social
world in the appropriate way.

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:03.980
- Excellent. Well, thanks
for taking us through.

00:15:03.980 --> 00:15:07.130
I know a lot of people are
going to be just enlightened

00:15:07.130 --> 00:15:08.070
with that information,

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:10.860
and it's great to have
that understanding again,

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:12.830
'cause it's come up in
so many different ways.

00:15:12.830 --> 00:15:15.150
And Pat, as someone who has expertise

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:17.380
both in neuroscience and in philosophy

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:19.370
or more appropriately in neuro philosophy,

00:15:19.370 --> 00:15:21.355
and he was also engaged in this,

00:15:21.355 --> 00:15:24.370
how has this information
informed your work

00:15:24.370 --> 00:15:26.840
and your conversations with people

00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:28.440
around these aspects of bonding?

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:34.120
- It's huge, of course,
because in one respect,

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:36.680
it really constitutes the platform

00:15:37.930 --> 00:15:40.280
for what we call morality.

00:15:40.280 --> 00:15:41.258
But I want to step back

00:15:41.258 --> 00:15:46.258
and because the sort
of bonding that we see

00:15:46.304 --> 00:15:49.603
in mammals and birds is really unique.

00:15:50.980 --> 00:15:52.740
And so you want to say,

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:56.820
well, because many people
have been very skeptical

00:15:56.820 --> 00:15:57.653
of the idea.

00:15:57.653 --> 00:15:59.580
Richard Dawkins actually is one

00:16:00.759 --> 00:16:04.790
that there could be genuine altruism

00:16:04.790 --> 00:16:05.930
that I could,

00:16:05.930 --> 00:16:07.370
as it were sacrificed,

00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:12.330
something that I want
for the good of another.

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:17.330
And so the idea is that
that could never evolve

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:20.620
because if there were
altruistic individuals,

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:25.082
always selfish guys would
take advantage of that's that.

00:16:25.082 --> 00:16:29.120
So part of what really motivated me

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:31.720
after I learned about the voles from Larry

00:16:31.720 --> 00:16:34.530
was why would this happen?

00:16:34.530 --> 00:16:37.453
Why would there be this kind of evolution?

00:16:38.381 --> 00:16:42.260
And I think the story
actually goes way, way back,

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:44.450
about 200 million years

00:16:44.450 --> 00:16:47.040
to the fact that warm bloodedness

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:48.790
appeared on the face of the planet.

00:16:49.830 --> 00:16:54.830
And the great thing
about being warm blooded

00:16:55.529 --> 00:16:58.100
is that you can forage at night.

00:16:58.100 --> 00:17:01.490
All those other guys have to
wait for the sun to come up

00:17:01.490 --> 00:17:03.210
but you can forage at night.

00:17:03.210 --> 00:17:07.510
But as with many sort
of changes in evolution,

00:17:07.510 --> 00:17:08.640
there's a downside.

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:11.500
And the downside is that gram for gram,

00:17:11.500 --> 00:17:15.550
a warm blooded animal has
to eat 10 times as much.

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:18.530
Now that's a huge evolutionary constraint.

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:20.040
As you know, you can go off

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:23.220
and leave your lizard alone for a week

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:27.350
and it's not going to be
starving when you get back.

00:17:27.350 --> 00:17:29.490
It doesn't need to eat that much.

00:17:29.490 --> 00:17:32.680
And also lizards and snakes
don't take care of their young.

00:17:32.680 --> 00:17:36.070
They just have them and off they go.

00:17:36.070 --> 00:17:39.240
So what happened such that mothers

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:43.480
and in any cases, fathers,
especially in birds,

00:17:43.480 --> 00:17:45.830
do this amazing thing,

00:17:45.830 --> 00:17:49.980
which has made this enormous
sacrifice for their offspring?

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:51.180
And the answer is this,

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:55.620
that to meet this ecological
constraint of eating so much,

00:17:55.620 --> 00:17:57.830
getting so much energy,

00:17:57.830 --> 00:18:02.270
basically mother nature
invented the cortex,

00:18:02.270 --> 00:18:05.720
and that's a way of letting the animals

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:08.730
learn a huge amount
about their environment,

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:12.680
and hence in a certain
sense, makes them smart.

00:18:12.680 --> 00:18:14.110
Birds have cortex too.

00:18:14.110 --> 00:18:15.330
It doesn't look like it.

00:18:15.330 --> 00:18:17.900
It's just a little different from ours.

00:18:17.900 --> 00:18:22.900
So, okay. But if you're going
to have cortex, then what?

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:25.410
It better not be already fully developed.

00:18:25.410 --> 00:18:28.230
It better be so immature

00:18:28.230 --> 00:18:33.130
that the baby can learn
from its environment.

00:18:33.130 --> 00:18:37.220
And that means the offspring
are born very, very immature.

00:18:37.220 --> 00:18:39.350
So then mother nature has a problem.

00:18:39.350 --> 00:18:41.680
Well, if they're that immature,

00:18:41.680 --> 00:18:42.770
they're not like a turtle,

00:18:42.770 --> 00:18:45.470
like they can just come out
of an egg and off they go.

00:18:46.840 --> 00:18:48.860
The baby mouse can't do anything

00:18:48.860 --> 00:18:51.890
it's deaf and blind inheritance.

00:18:51.890 --> 00:18:53.770
And we're pretty bad too.

00:18:53.770 --> 00:18:57.230
So you'll have to have
some sort of mechanism.

00:18:57.230 --> 00:19:01.030
And this is what obviously
evolved in mammals and birds,

00:19:01.030 --> 00:19:04.830
some sort of mechanism whereby
the thing that gives birth

00:19:04.830 --> 00:19:08.760
is also the thing that
begins to take care of it.

00:19:08.760 --> 00:19:12.360
And oxytocin, although it
was plentiful in the body

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:15.800
all over the place for
hundreds of millions of years,

00:19:15.800 --> 00:19:19.480
was put to a very new role.

00:19:19.480 --> 00:19:23.430
And that role had to do with
bonding and feeling good

00:19:23.430 --> 00:19:25.000
when you were with the baby,

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:27.520
feeling bad when the babies were gone

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:31.040
or if they were being
predated and so forth.

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:34.900
So that I think it puts morality

00:19:34.900 --> 00:19:38.480
into a completely different perspective

00:19:38.480 --> 00:19:42.310
from what most of the
evolutionary biologists

00:19:42.310 --> 00:19:44.700
had thought for so long.

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:49.000
Namely, that it has to
be beaten into kids.

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:51.430
No other animal has anything like it

00:19:52.680 --> 00:19:55.100
because it goes against evolution.

00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:58.490
And the answer is nuts.

00:19:58.490 --> 00:20:01.413
It is an evolutionary development.

00:20:02.550 --> 00:20:04.630
So there's more of course to the story.

00:20:04.630 --> 00:20:09.300
But I like the fact that
something as in direct

00:20:09.300 --> 00:20:12.210
shall we say, as being warm blooded

00:20:12.210 --> 00:20:16.170
turns out to be the essential part

00:20:16.170 --> 00:20:18.360
of the causal story for the emergence.

00:20:18.360 --> 00:20:21.124
So something like attachment and bonding,

00:20:21.124 --> 00:20:24.986
which is the essential
platform for morality.

00:20:24.986 --> 00:20:27.550
- Hmm. So much there.

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:29.847
And so many things that we
can pick up already too.

00:20:29.847 --> 00:20:31.940
And one thing I'd like
to just follow up on,

00:20:31.940 --> 00:20:34.340
'cause we started talking
about this developmental piece

00:20:34.340 --> 00:20:35.710
a little bit already too.

00:20:35.710 --> 00:20:37.260
So what are the implications of that?

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:38.630
'Cause you talked about the young

00:20:38.630 --> 00:20:42.290
not having that developed cortex yet

00:20:42.290 --> 00:20:44.240
and needing that bonding.

00:20:44.240 --> 00:20:46.080
So were the implications of that bonding

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:49.750
happening early on for life down the road

00:20:49.750 --> 00:20:52.940
and free to who wants to jump into that?

00:20:52.940 --> 00:20:54.367
- Well, I've just held for,

00:20:54.367 --> 00:20:57.000
so I'm going to let Larry start with that,

00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:00.050
but I'll talk about Allison
Perkeybile's work too.

00:21:00.050 --> 00:21:03.160
- Yeah. So we've done some
interesting work in the voles

00:21:03.160 --> 00:21:07.830
that show that if the pups experience

00:21:07.830 --> 00:21:08.830
a little bit of neglect,

00:21:08.830 --> 00:21:10.490
so they don't get as much licking

00:21:10.490 --> 00:21:12.110
and grooming from their mother.

00:21:12.110 --> 00:21:13.040
That licking and grooming

00:21:13.040 --> 00:21:15.570
causes oxytocin release in the brain.

00:21:15.570 --> 00:21:17.360
And if for the first two weeks of life,

00:21:17.360 --> 00:21:19.160
they get less of that,

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:20.150
then when they grow up,

00:21:20.150 --> 00:21:21.570
they actually have a difficulty

00:21:21.570 --> 00:21:24.550
forming relationships as adults.

00:21:24.550 --> 00:21:27.900
So this work suggests that as an infant,

00:21:27.900 --> 00:21:30.700
as the infant's brain is developing,

00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:33.730
the kind of nurturing that
it gets from the parents,

00:21:33.730 --> 00:21:38.690
the eye to eye contact,
the talking, the touching,

00:21:38.690 --> 00:21:42.830
all of that stuff is causing
a series of chemical events

00:21:42.830 --> 00:21:45.254
in the brain, such as
the release of oxytocin,

00:21:45.254 --> 00:21:48.496
that's help constructing the circuitry

00:21:48.496 --> 00:21:51.360
that determines how that individual

00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:54.597
is going to be able to relate
to others later in life.

00:21:54.597 --> 00:21:57.130
And I think that that's a huge,

00:21:57.130 --> 00:21:59.860
you had such huge societal implications

00:21:59.860 --> 00:22:03.010
because before knowing
this kind of research,

00:22:03.010 --> 00:22:04.510
people just think well, the baby is born,

00:22:04.510 --> 00:22:06.600
they're going to develop, however.

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:10.830
But their future child, your adult child

00:22:10.830 --> 00:22:14.795
really depends on how
you nurture that child.

00:22:14.795 --> 00:22:17.880
It's really, really critical.

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:20.250
- I think a part of the story

00:22:20.250 --> 00:22:22.007
that we haven't really touched on,

00:22:22.007 --> 00:22:24.050
that's so important here

00:22:24.050 --> 00:22:28.250
is that the other part of oxytocin,

00:22:28.250 --> 00:22:30.700
and that is the receptor on a neuron

00:22:30.700 --> 00:22:33.540
to which the oxytocin binds

00:22:33.540 --> 00:22:38.540
because what Larry and
his cohort discovered,

00:22:38.940 --> 00:22:43.920
was that it's the density
of oxytocin receptors

00:22:43.920 --> 00:22:47.010
in very specific parts of the brain

00:22:47.010 --> 00:22:48.935
that determines whether or not

00:22:48.935 --> 00:22:51.760
you're going to be a
long-term parabonder or not.

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:55.721
And similarly, we see that in some mammals

00:22:55.721 --> 00:23:00.530
there aren't male-female
bonds that are super strong,

00:23:00.530 --> 00:23:01.960
like in baboons.

00:23:01.960 --> 00:23:04.529
But what there are are matchlikes

00:23:04.529 --> 00:23:07.540
the head lady and her daughters

00:23:07.540 --> 00:23:10.890
and her daughters and her
daughters and so forth.

00:23:10.890 --> 00:23:13.630
And so we can see that just

00:23:13.630 --> 00:23:17.160
by the subtle alterations

00:23:17.160 --> 00:23:21.451
of the pattern of where
the oxytocin receptors are,

00:23:21.451 --> 00:23:23.490
you get changes.

00:23:23.490 --> 00:23:26.980
So to follow up on the developmental point

00:23:26.980 --> 00:23:28.360
that Larry was making,

00:23:28.360 --> 00:23:32.860
Allison Perkeybile's
show that when baby mice

00:23:32.860 --> 00:23:34.693
are taken from the mother

00:23:34.693 --> 00:23:38.560
and for even just a few hours a day,

00:23:38.560 --> 00:23:39.960
and they're not, like they're groomed,

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:41.440
but they're fed and kept warm

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:43.220
and then they're put back.

00:23:43.220 --> 00:23:48.220
What you see is a lowering
of the density of receptors

00:23:48.960 --> 00:23:50.770
in the places where you would expect it.

00:23:50.770 --> 00:23:52.890
And like Larry's work with voles,

00:23:52.890 --> 00:23:56.810
what she found was that when those animals

00:23:56.810 --> 00:24:00.450
then become mothers, they
are so to speak bad mothers.

00:24:00.450 --> 00:24:01.830
They don't really care that much.

00:24:01.830 --> 00:24:03.620
They don't bond much to their babies.

00:24:03.620 --> 00:24:07.740
I mean, they don't have the mechanism.

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:10.373
They don't have the
receptors for the oxytocin

00:24:10.373 --> 00:24:12.570
to latch onto.

00:24:12.570 --> 00:24:14.430
And so I agree with Larry,

00:24:14.430 --> 00:24:18.360
I think this is just
super, super important.

00:24:18.360 --> 00:24:20.380
I mean, we all knew
that it wasn't very good

00:24:20.380 --> 00:24:25.380
to put those Romanian orphans in cribs

00:24:25.600 --> 00:24:27.970
and leave them there for years.

00:24:27.970 --> 00:24:32.970
But we understand in a very direct way now

00:24:34.630 --> 00:24:36.450
why it was so terrible.

00:24:36.450 --> 00:24:37.370
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:24:37.370 --> 00:24:38.203
And that's so powerful.

00:24:38.203 --> 00:24:39.520
I mean, what you both mentioned,

00:24:39.520 --> 00:24:42.543
just having that understanding
of the brain mechanisms

00:24:42.543 --> 00:24:45.730
that impacts down the road.

00:24:45.730 --> 00:24:46.563
Go ahead, Larry.

00:24:46.563 --> 00:24:48.670
- Yeah. I was just saying,
we did another study,

00:24:48.670 --> 00:24:50.299
the first study that
I mentioned was about,

00:24:50.299 --> 00:24:53.561
when neglect, where the pups
were like sort of left alone

00:24:53.561 --> 00:24:54.900
for three hours a day.

00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:56.480
Only three hours a day,
that's not (indistinct).

00:24:56.480 --> 00:24:57.500
- I know, it's amazing.

00:24:57.500 --> 00:24:58.800
- But we also did experiments

00:24:58.800 --> 00:25:01.116
where they were raised by only the mom

00:25:01.116 --> 00:25:02.720
or the mom and the dad.

00:25:02.720 --> 00:25:04.410
And so in the case with only the mom,

00:25:04.410 --> 00:25:05.960
the mom had the forage for food

00:25:05.960 --> 00:25:07.780
and left the pups alone in the nest

00:25:07.780 --> 00:25:09.440
for a certain amount of time.

00:25:09.440 --> 00:25:10.820
Or when they were both parents were there,

00:25:10.820 --> 00:25:12.470
they were always had a parent

00:25:12.470 --> 00:25:15.840
that was engaged with the pups.

00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:19.290
Even the pups that were
raised only by the mother

00:25:19.290 --> 00:25:20.346
with just one parent,

00:25:20.346 --> 00:25:23.510
they had more difficulty
forming relationships

00:25:23.510 --> 00:25:24.990
later in life.

00:25:24.990 --> 00:25:28.310
So it is not just at the extremes.

00:25:28.310 --> 00:25:30.030
So I think the lesson from that-

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:31.773
- That's very interesting.

00:25:31.773 --> 00:25:35.699
- That it is the sort of the
total amount of nurturing

00:25:35.699 --> 00:25:38.770
that individuals give, that babies give,

00:25:38.770 --> 00:25:41.254
whether it comes from their grandmother

00:25:41.254 --> 00:25:44.540
from their aunt or from their father,

00:25:44.540 --> 00:25:45.473
or from the mother,

00:25:45.473 --> 00:25:48.370
all of that sort of
accumulative kind of thing.

00:25:48.370 --> 00:25:49.507
And you can think of it in your babies,

00:25:49.507 --> 00:25:51.459
this is a project that you're working on

00:25:51.459 --> 00:25:54.150
you're trying to build
a healthy individual

00:25:54.150 --> 00:25:56.487
for the next generation.

00:25:56.487 --> 00:25:58.830
- Absolutely. Yeah.

00:25:58.830 --> 00:26:00.940
That is a beautiful experiment, actually.

00:26:00.940 --> 00:26:01.920
I didn't know about that.

00:26:01.920 --> 00:26:03.560
- Yeah, definitely it's fascinating.

00:26:03.560 --> 00:26:04.740
And for those who are listening,

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:06.580
who may be hearing a lot of this,

00:26:06.580 --> 00:26:07.920
the science for the first time too,

00:26:07.920 --> 00:26:11.606
I imagine there's both
fascinating and intimidating

00:26:11.606 --> 00:26:12.780
at the same time,

00:26:12.780 --> 00:26:15.740
because even as you're describing
some of the experiments,

00:26:15.740 --> 00:26:18.063
one perspective could be
well if that's happened,

00:26:18.063 --> 00:26:19.600
does that mean that that person

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:24.180
is now set to have disrupted
relationships going forward?

00:26:24.180 --> 00:26:27.020
Or are there things that happened later on

00:26:27.020 --> 00:26:29.750
that can also still have
effects on the brain

00:26:29.750 --> 00:26:31.520
when we talk about changes
in the brain plasticity?

00:26:31.520 --> 00:26:35.462
So how do those processes
later on in life also intersect

00:26:35.462 --> 00:26:38.180
with some of these early differences

00:26:38.180 --> 00:26:39.013
that you've talked about?

00:26:39.013 --> 00:26:40.450
Whether someone, if we think about humans,

00:26:40.450 --> 00:26:42.970
if people have had neglect
in certain situations,

00:26:42.970 --> 00:26:43.860
is that something,

00:26:43.860 --> 00:26:45.110
are they stuck in that category?

00:26:45.110 --> 00:26:47.321
What happens down the road?

00:26:47.321 --> 00:26:48.154
- They're not.

00:26:48.154 --> 00:26:49.470
I don't think that they're
stuck in that category,

00:26:49.470 --> 00:26:52.290
but they have that trajectory.

00:26:52.290 --> 00:26:54.260
And if that trajectory isn't changed,

00:26:54.260 --> 00:26:55.811
that trajectory can change

00:26:55.811 --> 00:26:59.460
as they reach adolescence
or pass adolescence

00:26:59.460 --> 00:27:02.763
or even, as in adults.

00:27:04.520 --> 00:27:07.183
But it needs the attention to do that.

00:27:08.023 --> 00:27:11.100
Either that individual
or someone needs to say,

00:27:11.100 --> 00:27:13.365
okay, I need to engage in things

00:27:13.365 --> 00:27:15.320
that would put me in the right direction.

00:27:15.320 --> 00:27:18.270
And so it's just as long as we are aware.

00:27:18.270 --> 00:27:19.810
So, even genes.

00:27:19.810 --> 00:27:22.630
We often hear genes predict behavior,

00:27:22.630 --> 00:27:23.990
but having a gene doesn't mean

00:27:23.990 --> 00:27:26.810
that you have to engage in
that behavior that it predicts.

00:27:26.810 --> 00:27:29.530
It's just, these are all just vectors

00:27:29.530 --> 00:27:31.730
that sort of point people
in certain directions.

00:27:31.730 --> 00:27:32.940
And if left alone,

00:27:32.940 --> 00:27:35.390
they will continue to
go in that direction.

00:27:35.390 --> 00:27:37.170
But the knowledge that
these are deflectors

00:27:37.170 --> 00:27:38.750
that are pointing people in directions,

00:27:38.750 --> 00:27:41.210
and we know that that's
not the right direction,

00:27:41.210 --> 00:27:43.763
we should think of ways
to change the direction.

00:27:46.060 --> 00:27:47.650
'Cause it means,

00:27:47.650 --> 00:27:51.044
health and happiness all
the way through life,

00:27:51.044 --> 00:27:55.583
we live way past our
childhood and we don't wanna,

00:27:58.110 --> 00:28:00.040
if we had difficulty in childhood,

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:02.490
we don't want to be
stuck with the remnants

00:28:02.490 --> 00:28:03.410
of that difficulty.

00:28:03.410 --> 00:28:04.689
We want to fix those.

00:28:04.689 --> 00:28:07.000
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:28:07.000 --> 00:28:10.610
I sometimes think of
those small communities

00:28:10.610 --> 00:28:13.269
of hunter gatherers or people like,

00:28:13.269 --> 00:28:16.470
small groups of Inuits,

00:28:16.470 --> 00:28:21.270
maybe 10 or 20 living in a small community

00:28:21.270 --> 00:28:23.920
where somebody is always there.

00:28:23.920 --> 00:28:28.020
Somebody is always carrying
the baby in the hood.

00:28:28.020 --> 00:28:33.020
And I do think city life

00:28:33.280 --> 00:28:36.273
makes things a little more
difficult, shall we say.

00:28:37.674 --> 00:28:41.310
We don't always know the people next door.

00:28:41.310 --> 00:28:43.300
So we don't always pitch in

00:28:43.300 --> 00:28:44.860
when we know that the baby

00:28:44.860 --> 00:28:48.623
really needs a certain
amount of cuddling and care.

00:28:49.890 --> 00:28:51.900
- Yeah. Really well said.

00:28:51.900 --> 00:28:54.827
Well, I appreciate the context
that you've both put this in

00:28:54.827 --> 00:28:57.953
and with the biology of it as well.

00:28:57.953 --> 00:28:59.420
Anyway, I think of both of you

00:28:59.420 --> 00:29:01.300
as really excellent communicators

00:29:01.300 --> 00:29:03.820
and excellent scientific communicators

00:29:03.820 --> 00:29:05.870
in terms of taking this
knowledge that we have

00:29:05.870 --> 00:29:06.703
and making sure

00:29:06.703 --> 00:29:08.520
that it's part of the public discourse.

00:29:08.520 --> 00:29:10.140
Would you say that in terms of,

00:29:10.140 --> 00:29:11.597
I mean, everything that
you both have described

00:29:11.597 --> 00:29:14.730
and what we've learned about
the importance of these bonds,

00:29:14.730 --> 00:29:18.720
are we really paying attention
to that enough as a society?

00:29:18.720 --> 00:29:20.580
Or are there more things that we should do

00:29:20.580 --> 00:29:22.378
'cause Larry even talked about,

00:29:22.378 --> 00:29:24.100
if those things happen early on,

00:29:24.100 --> 00:29:24.933
we can change the direction.

00:29:24.933 --> 00:29:26.183
And I'm wondering even
what are some of the ways

00:29:26.183 --> 00:29:28.013
that we do change that trajectory?

00:29:29.127 --> 00:29:31.230
I know I just asked two
questions at once, but.

00:29:31.230 --> 00:29:32.685
- Yeah, I was thinking of the first one.

00:29:32.685 --> 00:29:34.330
It depends on the society

00:29:34.330 --> 00:29:36.098
because in many countries like Sweden,

00:29:36.098 --> 00:29:39.610
they have enacted laws and policies

00:29:39.610 --> 00:29:43.320
that really enhance
the parental engagement

00:29:43.320 --> 00:29:47.470
with paternal leave from work

00:29:47.470 --> 00:29:50.810
and longer time with the child.

00:29:50.810 --> 00:29:53.292
So in this country,

00:29:53.292 --> 00:29:54.906
we lag behind some other countries.

00:29:54.906 --> 00:29:56.570
But I think that we are,

00:29:56.570 --> 00:29:59.488
I mean, slowly gaining this understanding

00:29:59.488 --> 00:30:03.080
much more than we were
20, 40, 60 years ago.

00:30:03.080 --> 00:30:05.320
So we are making improvements.

00:30:05.320 --> 00:30:09.600
And as science goes forward,

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:10.700
this is the business we're in.

00:30:10.700 --> 00:30:11.909
Science is trying to figure out

00:30:11.909 --> 00:30:14.820
how things work and how
to make things better.

00:30:14.820 --> 00:30:17.270
I think that we are moving
in the right direction

00:30:18.320 --> 00:30:20.203
just by appreciating the biology.

00:30:21.970 --> 00:30:25.580
- I think the other part of the story too

00:30:25.580 --> 00:30:29.310
that needs emphasis is that

00:30:29.310 --> 00:30:32.380
it isn't just that the
child needs to be cuddled

00:30:32.380 --> 00:30:33.213
and so forth.

00:30:33.213 --> 00:30:36.120
It also, especially in humans,

00:30:36.120 --> 00:30:41.050
but also dogs and many
kinds of species of mammals.

00:30:41.050 --> 00:30:46.050
The infants are hugely
disposed to imitate others,

00:30:46.660 --> 00:30:48.944
adult siblings, and so forth.

00:30:48.944 --> 00:30:53.944
And how they learn the
norms of their group

00:30:54.730 --> 00:30:57.570
is from watching and interacting

00:30:57.570 --> 00:30:59.920
and doing things and being told, yes, yes,

00:30:59.920 --> 00:31:00.753
that's great.

00:31:00.753 --> 00:31:03.530
Or no, you don't want
to do that and so forth.

00:31:03.530 --> 00:31:05.990
And this is a part of the story

00:31:05.990 --> 00:31:09.210
that engages a very ancient system.

00:31:09.210 --> 00:31:10.990
And that is the reward system,

00:31:10.990 --> 00:31:13.470
which is largely subcortical,

00:31:13.470 --> 00:31:16.660
but of course has very dense interactions

00:31:16.660 --> 00:31:18.260
with cortex as well.

00:31:18.260 --> 00:31:21.000
And so it's really important

00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:24.960
not only that the children
are loved and nurtured,

00:31:24.960 --> 00:31:27.470
but they also have the opportunities

00:31:27.470 --> 00:31:29.910
to interact in social ways

00:31:29.910 --> 00:31:31.850
so that they come to understand

00:31:31.850 --> 00:31:36.350
that on the whole, truth
telling is better than Fitbit.

00:31:36.350 --> 00:31:41.350
And that disagreements
can best be resolved

00:31:41.360 --> 00:31:43.020
sometimes by working it out,

00:31:43.020 --> 00:31:47.086
rather than by bonking somebody
on the noggin and so on.

00:31:47.086 --> 00:31:51.500
But they need to pick these things up.

00:31:51.500 --> 00:31:54.510
And if they are raised in circumstances

00:31:54.510 --> 00:31:56.290
that are really grim,

00:31:56.290 --> 00:32:00.500
they're going to pick up
very different kinds of norms

00:32:00.500 --> 00:32:05.163
and then it's going to
be hard to undo those.

00:32:06.340 --> 00:32:11.340
And so I think that's a
reason why family life

00:32:12.170 --> 00:32:15.100
and extended family life
are really very important.

00:32:15.100 --> 00:32:18.260
Why grandparents have an
important role to play

00:32:18.260 --> 00:32:22.670
and as well as siblings and
aunts and uncles and neighbors

00:32:22.670 --> 00:32:25.260
and dogs.

00:32:25.260 --> 00:32:27.850
So yeah, it is.

00:32:27.850 --> 00:32:30.110
I mean, dogs teach kids a lot

00:32:31.090 --> 00:32:34.989
and that's their learning norms

00:32:34.989 --> 00:32:39.680
that most of us can't
even articulate even now,

00:32:39.680 --> 00:32:42.403
but we know, we know.

00:32:43.620 --> 00:32:44.930
- Yeah, that's so important.

00:32:44.930 --> 00:32:49.040
And again, it seems that
there's just a lot of bonus

00:32:49.040 --> 00:32:52.345
on each member of the
family and the community

00:32:52.345 --> 00:32:53.970
in so many different ways.

00:32:53.970 --> 00:32:54.803
I mean, I'm wondering also,

00:32:54.803 --> 00:32:57.000
just from a philosophical perspective,

00:32:57.000 --> 00:32:59.710
what does that say about us as humans

00:32:59.710 --> 00:33:02.720
and the importance of altruism?

00:33:02.720 --> 00:33:04.105
I mean, as we have learned about

00:33:04.105 --> 00:33:06.420
all these different outcomes

00:33:06.420 --> 00:33:08.250
and effects that these
interaction is going to have

00:33:08.250 --> 00:33:09.310
on our fellow human beings.

00:33:09.310 --> 00:33:10.663
What responsibility would you say

00:33:10.663 --> 00:33:13.600
that has for us as fellow humans?

00:33:13.600 --> 00:33:14.463
- Well, I think,

00:33:15.470 --> 00:33:17.930
even in small hunter gatherer groups,

00:33:17.930 --> 00:33:20.940
people understand in a very deep way

00:33:20.940 --> 00:33:24.039
the importance of cooperation
and mutual defense

00:33:24.039 --> 00:33:26.575
and so forth.

00:33:26.575 --> 00:33:31.575
And I think that children are
very quick to pick that up.

00:33:33.630 --> 00:33:38.250
We also see in cross species alliances,

00:33:38.250 --> 00:33:41.600
for example, between wolves and humans

00:33:41.600 --> 00:33:45.850
or between wolves and gelada baboons

00:33:45.850 --> 00:33:49.500
that there can be very close relationships

00:33:49.500 --> 00:33:52.490
because they cooperate with each other.

00:33:52.490 --> 00:33:54.500
And that's really valuable

00:33:54.500 --> 00:33:57.110
in the case of us and the baboons,

00:33:57.110 --> 00:33:58.430
the wolves are very important

00:33:58.430 --> 00:34:00.463
in helping to drive off predators.

00:34:02.088 --> 00:34:06.689
And in return, the baboons tolerate them,

00:34:06.689 --> 00:34:08.550
they don't snuggle with them

00:34:08.550 --> 00:34:10.170
in the way we snuggle with dogs.

00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:12.740
But maybe it took us a
few generations as well

00:34:12.740 --> 00:34:13.573
I don't know.

00:34:15.620 --> 00:34:18.110
- Well, I think one of the
interesting things about altruism

00:34:18.110 --> 00:34:20.170
is that we've learned from,

00:34:20.170 --> 00:34:23.250
well, in work with
animals but also in humans

00:34:23.250 --> 00:34:28.250
is that we tend to think
of others in our species

00:34:28.670 --> 00:34:31.490
as either within our group or out-group.

00:34:31.490 --> 00:34:34.520
And so we exhibit altruistic behavior

00:34:34.520 --> 00:34:36.610
towards those that are in our group.

00:34:36.610 --> 00:34:37.900
And altruistic behavior

00:34:37.900 --> 00:34:40.480
is someone needs help and you help them,

00:34:40.480 --> 00:34:41.780
you detect that they need the help,

00:34:41.780 --> 00:34:43.250
and then you help them.

00:34:43.250 --> 00:34:44.230
And I think that evolved

00:34:44.230 --> 00:34:46.510
from the brain mechanisms
of maternal behavior,

00:34:46.510 --> 00:34:48.791
because mothers are always altruistic.

00:34:48.791 --> 00:34:52.796
They can hear their pup being
in distress or their baby,

00:34:52.796 --> 00:34:55.800
a Wildebeest can be as can see their baby

00:34:55.800 --> 00:34:57.900
being approached by a lion,

00:34:57.900 --> 00:35:01.800
then they'll risk their
life to help their baby.

00:35:01.800 --> 00:35:02.633
But I think in humans

00:35:02.633 --> 00:35:05.500
the same neuro-biological processes

00:35:05.500 --> 00:35:09.210
have been expanded to others in our group

00:35:09.210 --> 00:35:10.670
because when we help our group,

00:35:10.670 --> 00:35:11.873
our group does better.

00:35:12.760 --> 00:35:15.710
The problem is, is who
do we see as in our group

00:35:15.710 --> 00:35:18.470
and who do we see outside of our group?

00:35:18.470 --> 00:35:23.470
And I think that those divisions
of who's us and who's not,

00:35:25.080 --> 00:35:29.880
I mean, you can see it in
politics right now, us and them.

00:35:29.880 --> 00:35:32.810
And so that kind of division

00:35:32.810 --> 00:35:35.570
was something that we
really need to grapple with

00:35:35.570 --> 00:35:40.570
how to erase those boundaries and sort of,

00:35:41.550 --> 00:35:45.263
so that the group becomes all of us.

00:35:47.330 --> 00:35:49.794
- And increasingly I think,

00:35:49.794 --> 00:35:54.070
people are recognizing that

00:35:55.120 --> 00:35:59.030
because the planet isn't
an infinitely large place,

00:35:59.030 --> 00:36:03.490
and because things like
climate affect us all,

00:36:03.490 --> 00:36:08.010
that we do need to cooperate
with people that we don't know

00:36:08.010 --> 00:36:10.870
and maybe never see and so forth.

00:36:10.870 --> 00:36:13.860
Nevertheless, I think the strongest,

00:36:13.860 --> 00:36:16.830
I think this is almost
certainly to remain the case,

00:36:16.830 --> 00:36:21.810
but the strongest desire for help

00:36:21.810 --> 00:36:25.193
and reaching out and so
forth, is going to be local.

00:36:26.190 --> 00:36:28.570
I mean, there is a group of philosophers

00:36:28.570 --> 00:36:29.740
who say that's wrong,

00:36:29.740 --> 00:36:31.720
that it should always be

00:36:31.720 --> 00:36:34.810
the person who's most needy
wherever they are on the planet.

00:36:34.810 --> 00:36:38.780
And like I have this friend
who cuts people's hair.

00:36:38.780 --> 00:36:41.130
I mean, she's a hairdresser, I guess.

00:36:41.130 --> 00:36:43.720
And a couple of times a month,

00:36:43.720 --> 00:36:46.130
she takes her tools and chair

00:36:46.130 --> 00:36:49.180
and goes down and cuts the
hair of homeless people.

00:36:49.180 --> 00:36:51.360
And maybe they don't need her time

00:36:51.360 --> 00:36:54.970
as much as somebody else on
the other side of the planet.

00:36:54.970 --> 00:36:59.970
But I think it's asking
too much to ask her

00:37:00.088 --> 00:37:02.960
to figure out who really needs her help

00:37:02.960 --> 00:37:04.530
and to quit running downtown

00:37:04.530 --> 00:37:07.620
to cut the hair of the homeless people.

00:37:07.620 --> 00:37:12.380
On the other hand, where I
absolutely agree with Larry

00:37:12.380 --> 00:37:16.850
is that we don't want to let localness

00:37:16.850 --> 00:37:21.850
kind of be the enemy of
looking further a field

00:37:21.920 --> 00:37:24.810
when it's really important to do so.

00:37:24.810 --> 00:37:29.810
And so we have to both be
motivated as we all are

00:37:32.100 --> 00:37:33.483
when it's somebody we know.

00:37:34.940 --> 00:37:38.150
But we also don't want to have

00:37:38.150 --> 00:37:40.970
the sort of in-group thing

00:37:40.970 --> 00:37:43.710
where we're the good guys
and they're the bad guys.

00:37:43.710 --> 00:37:45.688
That's when the trouble starts.

00:37:45.688 --> 00:37:46.810
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:37:46.810 --> 00:37:48.440
So many important pieces there.

00:37:48.440 --> 00:37:51.550
And I love how you've tied
in that aspect of altruism

00:37:51.550 --> 00:37:53.060
and just what the science has showed us

00:37:53.060 --> 00:37:56.221
about the within group
versus the without group.

00:37:56.221 --> 00:37:58.630
So many directions I could go

00:37:58.630 --> 00:38:00.780
and I'm formulating the ideas

00:38:00.780 --> 00:38:02.010
as I talk through this.

00:38:02.010 --> 00:38:03.407
So bear with me as we go through it.

00:38:03.407 --> 00:38:04.240
But I mean, that's just something

00:38:04.240 --> 00:38:07.010
that we've thought a lot
about on this podcast,

00:38:07.010 --> 00:38:09.400
as well as we've talked about
aspects of social justice.

00:38:09.400 --> 00:38:11.430
And even Larry, as you were talking,

00:38:11.430 --> 00:38:12.940
it reminded me of those components

00:38:12.940 --> 00:38:15.750
through thinking who is within
the group and who is outside

00:38:15.750 --> 00:38:18.170
and the benefits of helping
those within our group.

00:38:18.170 --> 00:38:20.310
But then also on the flip side,

00:38:20.310 --> 00:38:23.170
the detriments of things
that happen within the group.

00:38:23.170 --> 00:38:24.770
So I've referenced a couple of times,

00:38:24.770 --> 00:38:26.608
some of David Williams work at Harvard

00:38:26.608 --> 00:38:29.810
looking specifically at
black and brown communities

00:38:29.810 --> 00:38:33.040
and the long-term detrimental
mental health effects

00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:35.280
when there are individuals
in those communities

00:38:35.280 --> 00:38:36.510
who are killed at the hands of police

00:38:36.510 --> 00:38:37.343
when they're unharmed.

00:38:37.343 --> 00:38:40.587
So the way it goes both ways.

00:38:40.587 --> 00:38:43.490
And so even as we're talking
about this just makes me think,

00:38:43.490 --> 00:38:46.800
and not that there's
evidence for this per se,

00:38:46.800 --> 00:38:50.540
but the power of expanding those groups

00:38:50.540 --> 00:38:53.400
and what that would do for
us collectively as a society,

00:38:53.400 --> 00:38:56.420
whether there would be some of
these aspects of the bonding

00:38:56.420 --> 00:38:57.253
that would happen from
biological perspective

00:38:57.253 --> 00:38:59.700
that would have long-term
effects even in adults,

00:38:59.700 --> 00:39:00.610
even though we're passing.

00:39:00.610 --> 00:39:01.790
- Yeah, no, I agree.

00:39:01.790 --> 00:39:04.530
I mean, and there is
some work on chimpanzees

00:39:06.117 --> 00:39:10.770
where the fielded servers
were watching two groups

00:39:10.770 --> 00:39:12.760
because they were both
getting rather large,

00:39:12.760 --> 00:39:15.130
and they rather suspected
that there was going to be

00:39:15.130 --> 00:39:18.537
a bit of a fight eventually,

00:39:18.537 --> 00:39:19.940
which there was,

00:39:19.940 --> 00:39:22.160
but they managed to collect urine samples

00:39:22.160 --> 00:39:27.160
from the members of the group
before and after the incident.

00:39:29.860 --> 00:39:32.223
The oxytocin levels had
gone through the roof.

00:39:33.190 --> 00:39:36.080
These guys were, they
were all of a sudden,

00:39:36.080 --> 00:39:38.010
so bonded to each other.

00:39:38.010 --> 00:39:40.310
And also probably, I mean,

00:39:40.310 --> 00:39:41.960
Larry could speak to this better than I,

00:39:41.960 --> 00:39:44.777
their stress levels had gone down.

00:39:44.777 --> 00:39:47.480
And so they weren't quite as afraid.

00:39:47.480 --> 00:39:50.360
And boy did they go at it.

00:39:50.360 --> 00:39:52.580
And that's very biological.

00:39:52.580 --> 00:39:55.290
Now are humans like that?

00:39:55.290 --> 00:39:58.720
It would surprise me if we
didn't see it, for example,

00:39:58.720 --> 00:40:02.900
in football teams or basketball teams,

00:40:02.900 --> 00:40:04.550
maybe baseball's too slow for it.

00:40:07.077 --> 00:40:08.327
- Yeah, you certainly do.

00:40:09.374 --> 00:40:11.630
If you give someone oxytocin

00:40:11.630 --> 00:40:14.883
and you tell them that these
people are on your team,

00:40:14.883 --> 00:40:18.067
you're more likely to do
something altruistic to them

00:40:18.067 --> 00:40:20.440
and maybe be more defensive

00:40:20.440 --> 00:40:24.185
to someone who is taught the
word old is not on your team.

00:40:24.185 --> 00:40:26.049
And so, I think that-

00:40:26.049 --> 00:40:27.609
- That's a better deal.

00:40:27.609 --> 00:40:30.319
- It's a very difficult thing

00:40:30.319 --> 00:40:34.863
in terms of racism in the
society and to fix it right away.

00:40:35.765 --> 00:40:38.539
It's not going to be an
instant fix of course,

00:40:38.539 --> 00:40:41.459
but if by thinking about this,

00:40:41.459 --> 00:40:44.730
the biology of responding
to in-groups and out-groups,

00:40:44.730 --> 00:40:45.960
what we need to do is to try

00:40:45.960 --> 00:40:50.070
to think of ways, move forward in ways

00:40:50.070 --> 00:40:55.070
so that our skin color is not a factor

00:40:55.560 --> 00:40:57.910
to determine in-group versus out-group.

00:40:57.910 --> 00:41:00.761
And that can be done

00:41:00.761 --> 00:41:05.451
through making sure that
the diversity is represented

00:41:05.451 --> 00:41:07.450
in all aspects of our lives.

00:41:07.450 --> 00:41:09.863
So we can see, whatever we are doing,

00:41:12.530 --> 00:41:15.000
there's a lot of different kinds of people

00:41:15.000 --> 00:41:16.007
that are also like that.

00:41:16.007 --> 00:41:18.030
And rather than...

00:41:18.980 --> 00:41:21.630
And it just takes time to reach everybody

00:41:21.630 --> 00:41:24.300
in that kind of way.

00:41:24.300 --> 00:41:25.160
- Yeah.

00:41:25.160 --> 00:41:27.230
- I think that's probably true.

00:41:27.230 --> 00:41:30.946
I mean, I would love for
these things to move faster,

00:41:30.946 --> 00:41:33.990
but I'm always gratified

00:41:33.990 --> 00:41:36.550
when I see how, for example,

00:41:36.550 --> 00:41:40.270
sportscasters and newscasters in general,

00:41:40.270 --> 00:41:44.255
how it used to be your basic white male

00:41:44.255 --> 00:41:48.280
and now there's women,
there's people of color,

00:41:48.280 --> 00:41:50.210
there's all kinds.

00:41:50.210 --> 00:41:55.157
And because people watch
television so much,

00:41:55.157 --> 00:42:00.157
I mean, maybe I'm just
being foolishly optimistic,

00:42:00.170 --> 00:42:02.781
but I think those things matter,

00:42:02.781 --> 00:42:05.630
and I think it is making a difference.

00:42:05.630 --> 00:42:08.506
But we've got a long way to go.

00:42:08.506 --> 00:42:09.980
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:42:09.980 --> 00:42:10.813
Well, I appreciate the way

00:42:10.813 --> 00:42:12.220
that you both framed that as well.

00:42:12.220 --> 00:42:13.574
And then even as we've been talking,

00:42:13.574 --> 00:42:15.330
thinking back to some
of the earlier comments

00:42:15.330 --> 00:42:16.440
that we had in the conversation too,

00:42:16.440 --> 00:42:17.273
it also makes me wonder,

00:42:17.273 --> 00:42:19.040
and this is getting a little speculative,

00:42:19.040 --> 00:42:22.830
but just about those
bonds early in life too,

00:42:22.830 --> 00:42:23.663
early in childhood,

00:42:23.663 --> 00:42:25.860
if there are windows of opportunity

00:42:25.860 --> 00:42:28.510
to expand that circle in a sense,

00:42:28.510 --> 00:42:29.493
like you were talking about Larry.

00:42:29.493 --> 00:42:31.120
Is that bonds formed earlier,

00:42:31.120 --> 00:42:32.620
and that there's less of this,

00:42:33.460 --> 00:42:37.630
we and them, us and them kind
of framework from early on.

00:42:37.630 --> 00:42:39.270
And whether, from a
biological perspective,

00:42:39.270 --> 00:42:42.478
whether that would also
have long-term effects

00:42:42.478 --> 00:42:44.550
as people become adults.

00:42:44.550 --> 00:42:45.460
And again, I'm speculating,

00:42:45.460 --> 00:42:46.890
but just as we're having this conversation

00:42:46.890 --> 00:42:50.140
and thinking through some
of those topics as well.

00:42:50.140 --> 00:42:50.973
- Yeah, I think so.

00:42:50.973 --> 00:42:52.589
I mean, there was some work done in rats

00:42:52.589 --> 00:42:56.950
where you have rats that have
the different patterns of fur

00:42:58.060 --> 00:43:02.260
and if they are raised with other rats

00:43:02.260 --> 00:43:05.390
that have a different
color pattern of fur,

00:43:05.390 --> 00:43:08.960
then they were more likely to
show helpful behavior to them.

00:43:08.960 --> 00:43:13.010
So early learning is important.

00:43:13.010 --> 00:43:13.843
- [Patricia] I think so.

00:43:13.843 --> 00:43:14.676
- And so, I think

00:43:14.676 --> 00:43:16.994
this is just something
that we as a society

00:43:16.994 --> 00:43:19.410
have to recognize as important

00:43:19.410 --> 00:43:21.320
and just pay attention to that

00:43:21.320 --> 00:43:26.320
and increase exposure
to all kinds of people

00:43:30.399 --> 00:43:33.139
so that the skin color
and all of these things

00:43:33.139 --> 00:43:36.830
nationality or ethnic background

00:43:36.830 --> 00:43:41.830
are not the divisors of groups.

00:43:44.939 --> 00:43:49.020
- Yeah, no, I remember when I
was at first grade in school,

00:43:49.020 --> 00:43:50.698
again, this is in this tiny little school

00:43:50.698 --> 00:43:52.410
in British Columbia.

00:43:52.410 --> 00:43:56.090
And there was an influx of kids

00:43:56.090 --> 00:44:00.895
who wore leather pants that
were cut off above the knees.

00:44:00.895 --> 00:44:03.990
And of course what had happened

00:44:03.990 --> 00:44:06.538
was that there were a number of Germans

00:44:06.538 --> 00:44:11.538
who were moved to this area to farm

00:44:11.560 --> 00:44:14.158
and start a new life after the war.

00:44:14.158 --> 00:44:18.607
And we were all absolutely
fascinated by this.

00:44:18.607 --> 00:44:21.730
And of course, by the fact
that they didn't talk like us.

00:44:21.730 --> 00:44:24.380
But when you're only six,

00:44:24.380 --> 00:44:27.100
these things don't matter very much.

00:44:27.100 --> 00:44:31.380
So within I don't know,
six months or whatever

00:44:31.380 --> 00:44:33.000
it was all kind of,

00:44:33.000 --> 00:44:34.640
they were still wearing the leather pants

00:44:34.640 --> 00:44:37.480
'cause I think they
couldn't afford other pants.

00:44:37.480 --> 00:44:38.890
But it didn't matter anymore.

00:44:38.890 --> 00:44:42.320
And kids are much more
easy about these things

00:44:42.320 --> 00:44:47.320
and adults were kind of
ossified a little bit,

00:44:48.240 --> 00:44:49.620
I suppose should say.

00:44:49.620 --> 00:44:50.950
- Yeah, yeah.

00:44:50.950 --> 00:44:53.616
Again, sure, that ties back
to the biology as well.

00:44:53.616 --> 00:44:55.741
- I think, I think.

00:44:55.741 --> 00:44:56.620
- (indistinct) The learning.

00:44:56.620 --> 00:45:01.360
So lots that we could
learn from the younger ones

00:45:01.360 --> 00:45:02.473
among us as well.

00:45:04.210 --> 00:45:05.043
So as we wrap up,

00:45:05.043 --> 00:45:06.720
I was also just going to ask a little bit

00:45:06.720 --> 00:45:09.260
about some of the biology
that we've been touching on,

00:45:09.260 --> 00:45:11.410
but of consolation behavior.

00:45:11.410 --> 00:45:13.800
And then in this mode that we're in

00:45:13.800 --> 00:45:16.573
of kind of the ups and
downs of the pandemic,

00:45:17.840 --> 00:45:20.102
what that has also meant for us

00:45:20.102 --> 00:45:22.900
as there haven't been as
the same types of ways

00:45:22.900 --> 00:45:25.190
to express that consolatory behavior.

00:45:25.190 --> 00:45:26.023
And if there are ways

00:45:26.023 --> 00:45:28.693
that can also inform how
we interact moving forward,

00:45:28.693 --> 00:45:30.110
because Larry, as you mentioned,

00:45:30.110 --> 00:45:33.770
this is not something that's
going to be gone anytime soon.

00:45:33.770 --> 00:45:35.900
So Larry, I guess we could start with you

00:45:35.900 --> 00:45:37.860
as you've studied some of that as well.

00:45:37.860 --> 00:45:39.100
- Yeah. I mean, I think,

00:45:39.100 --> 00:45:40.730
so you mentioned consolation

00:45:40.730 --> 00:45:43.510
that this is something that
we studied in the voles

00:45:43.510 --> 00:45:46.370
where we see that if an
animal's partner is distressed

00:45:46.370 --> 00:45:47.794
or someone they know is distressed,

00:45:47.794 --> 00:45:49.554
they release the oxytocin

00:45:49.554 --> 00:45:52.114
and it makes them be
tuned into that partner

00:45:52.114 --> 00:45:53.840
and recognize that they're distressed

00:45:53.840 --> 00:45:55.610
and then do something.

00:45:55.610 --> 00:45:59.260
And I think that we're organized

00:45:59.260 --> 00:46:01.580
in such a way that we can detect others

00:46:01.580 --> 00:46:02.640
when they're in distress

00:46:02.640 --> 00:46:06.870
and have some urge to help them.

00:46:06.870 --> 00:46:09.947
And maybe we don't do that as much,

00:46:09.947 --> 00:46:12.330
when we're isolated and things like that.

00:46:12.330 --> 00:46:14.303
But I think just being aware

00:46:14.303 --> 00:46:16.303
that this is something that we should do

00:46:17.220 --> 00:46:20.940
that doing that, it's
the right thing to do.

00:46:20.940 --> 00:46:23.770
Perhaps someone recognize
when someone is in distress,

00:46:23.770 --> 00:46:27.310
it's also hard for us to know
when people are in distress.

00:46:27.310 --> 00:46:29.870
We see them once in a while on Zoom,

00:46:29.870 --> 00:46:32.920
we don't know what's going
on when they're alone.

00:46:32.920 --> 00:46:35.173
And just to kind of be aware of,

00:46:35.173 --> 00:46:40.173
ask people, how are you
doing and reach out to them

00:46:41.130 --> 00:46:44.633
to help if they need help.

00:46:47.375 --> 00:46:50.460
- Pat, anything you want
to add to that as well?

00:46:50.460 --> 00:46:53.076
- Well, I was just
thinking of other species,

00:46:53.076 --> 00:46:56.490
there was wonderful data that (indistinct)

00:46:56.490 --> 00:47:01.490
has had chimpanzees
consoling other chimpanzees

00:47:03.150 --> 00:47:08.030
and in the wild as well as in captivity.

00:47:08.030 --> 00:47:11.530
But of course dogs are
incredible in that way too.

00:47:11.530 --> 00:47:14.320
If you're upset or you're tearful

00:47:14.320 --> 00:47:17.400
or you've fallen or,

00:47:17.400 --> 00:47:20.400
the dog will come and they'll
nozzle and they'll lick,

00:47:20.400 --> 00:47:21.919
and if it's a baby, I mean,

00:47:21.919 --> 00:47:26.919
this used to be just
waved off as anecdotes.

00:47:27.140 --> 00:47:32.060
But now there's so much real data

00:47:32.060 --> 00:47:35.750
as well as lots and lots
of anecdotes on YouTube,

00:47:35.750 --> 00:47:37.283
but it's hard to dismiss.

00:47:38.149 --> 00:47:40.920
- I think one thing that has
happened during this pandemic

00:47:40.920 --> 00:47:42.240
is that people have realized

00:47:42.240 --> 00:47:45.870
how much of a lifeline
that their pets are.

00:47:45.870 --> 00:47:47.680
- Yes, absolutely.

00:47:47.680 --> 00:47:51.260
Because they really
have bonded emotionally

00:47:51.260 --> 00:47:52.093
with their pets.

00:47:52.093 --> 00:47:54.680
And their pets are a source of joy

00:47:54.680 --> 00:47:56.090
and that's really social interaction

00:47:56.090 --> 00:47:58.210
that's even as others.

00:47:58.210 --> 00:48:00.980
And so that's something we sort of,

00:48:00.980 --> 00:48:03.310
in the absence of other interactions,

00:48:03.310 --> 00:48:06.800
we sort of built and strengthen that.

00:48:06.800 --> 00:48:10.023
And that can help improve mental health.

00:48:10.871 --> 00:48:12.560
But I do think that people

00:48:14.110 --> 00:48:19.110
eventually need to wean
themselves off of only that.

00:48:19.567 --> 00:48:22.450
(all laughing loudly)

00:48:22.450 --> 00:48:25.631
Because having real
human social interactions

00:48:25.631 --> 00:48:30.631
is so important for so many other things.

00:48:32.300 --> 00:48:34.710
So yeah.

00:48:34.710 --> 00:48:36.180
- One thing I've often wondered,

00:48:36.180 --> 00:48:38.220
and I doubt there's any way
that anyone's looked at this,

00:48:38.220 --> 00:48:40.300
it's just, some of the things
that you've talked about

00:48:40.300 --> 00:48:42.750
in terms of the oxytocin being released

00:48:42.750 --> 00:48:43.860
in certain interactions

00:48:43.860 --> 00:48:45.530
and how that can also build trust,

00:48:45.530 --> 00:48:48.310
whether that can also happen on Zoom,

00:48:48.310 --> 00:48:50.320
if it happens to the lesser of.

00:48:50.320 --> 00:48:52.030
- Yeah, that's a big deal.

00:48:52.030 --> 00:48:54.840
- I know people have been
talking about that specifically.

00:48:54.840 --> 00:48:56.882
- Yeah, yeah. That is a big deal.

00:48:56.882 --> 00:48:59.327
- People have been talking about that

00:48:59.327 --> 00:49:01.960
before Zoom, before the pandemic,

00:49:01.960 --> 00:49:03.753
as people started texting.

00:49:03.753 --> 00:49:08.753
So children who are with their mother

00:49:09.040 --> 00:49:10.010
and hear the mother's voice

00:49:10.010 --> 00:49:12.130
or their father engaging with them.

00:49:12.130 --> 00:49:14.390
They have big peak of oxytocin,

00:49:14.390 --> 00:49:17.163
but if you get a text, you don't.

00:49:18.200 --> 00:49:21.930
And I'm sure that the oxytocin
release that you hear.

00:49:21.930 --> 00:49:24.270
I mean, even if you're just
interacting with another person

00:49:24.270 --> 00:49:25.660
and it's a positive interaction,

00:49:25.660 --> 00:49:28.940
the eye to eye contact is
causing oxytocin release.

00:49:28.940 --> 00:49:29.773
And that's like,

00:49:29.773 --> 00:49:32.090
it's kind of like exercise
in your social brain.

00:49:32.090 --> 00:49:33.810
You need that from a day to day basis.

00:49:33.810 --> 00:49:36.900
And when you only get the
interactions through Zoom,

00:49:36.900 --> 00:49:39.900
I don't think you're having
that same kind of interactions.

00:49:39.900 --> 00:49:41.240
You don't feel the connections.

00:49:41.240 --> 00:49:44.513
You're a 2D image that moves.

00:49:46.256 --> 00:49:50.830
And yeah, so that's having
long-term impacts on them.

00:49:52.160 --> 00:49:54.120
- There's was an additional thing here,

00:49:54.120 --> 00:49:57.240
which is that oxytocin has

00:49:57.240 --> 00:50:01.400
what is sometimes referred
to as a sibling peptide,

00:50:01.400 --> 00:50:03.570
which is vasopressin.

00:50:03.570 --> 00:50:08.570
And sometimes the release of vasopressin

00:50:08.600 --> 00:50:13.600
can have to do with being
careful, anxiety, caution,

00:50:15.100 --> 00:50:15.980
and so forth.

00:50:15.980 --> 00:50:19.270
And it turns out that this
is work by Sue Carter,

00:50:19.270 --> 00:50:24.270
that oxytocin can bind
to vasopressin receptors

00:50:24.630 --> 00:50:26.800
with the upshot that there can be,

00:50:26.800 --> 00:50:31.350
yeah, well, you know, yes, but maybe not.

00:50:31.350 --> 00:50:33.710
And when I first read her paper,

00:50:33.710 --> 00:50:37.700
I thought about some of the
male-female encounters one has,

00:50:37.700 --> 00:50:39.737
especially for example, as a teenager.

00:50:39.737 --> 00:50:41.570
Is he really being mean?

00:50:41.570 --> 00:50:42.680
Or is that a joke?

00:50:42.680 --> 00:50:45.697
Or I kind of liked him, but maybe not.

00:50:45.697 --> 00:50:50.130
But I think those sorts
of complex dynamics,

00:50:50.130 --> 00:50:53.910
those ambiguities are often in play

00:50:53.910 --> 00:50:57.990
even when we are
contemplating helping someone

00:50:57.990 --> 00:51:02.170
or wondering whether we
should give them shelter

00:51:02.170 --> 00:51:03.010
and so forth.

00:51:03.010 --> 00:51:04.207
Is it been (indistinct).

00:51:07.354 --> 00:51:08.867
- You have to be vigilant on who you help.

00:51:08.867 --> 00:51:09.700
- You have to be.

00:51:09.700 --> 00:51:11.476
- And there's interesting work on rats

00:51:11.476 --> 00:51:14.340
showing that an adult rat,

00:51:14.340 --> 00:51:19.340
if they have a choice to
interact with a juvenile rat

00:51:19.550 --> 00:51:20.750
that's in distress

00:51:20.750 --> 00:51:23.780
versus another adult
male that's in distress,

00:51:23.780 --> 00:51:26.927
they avoid that adult
male that's in distress.

00:51:26.927 --> 00:51:27.760
And they help the female

00:51:27.760 --> 00:51:32.560
because an adult who is in
distress could be (indistinct)

00:51:33.556 --> 00:51:36.600
and that is also regulated by oxytocin-

00:51:36.600 --> 00:51:38.303
- And vasopressin.

00:51:38.303 --> 00:51:40.580
- These brain mechanisms.

00:51:40.580 --> 00:51:44.970
So many of the things that
we engage in every day

00:51:44.970 --> 00:51:47.980
that we don't even think about
what's going on in the brain.

00:51:47.980 --> 00:51:50.705
We're starting to understand the hormones,

00:51:50.705 --> 00:51:53.050
the chemicals in the
brain that's doing it,

00:51:53.050 --> 00:51:54.780
but also now the circuitry,

00:51:54.780 --> 00:51:56.890
we're learning more and
more about the circuitry

00:51:56.890 --> 00:51:57.920
of how it's working

00:51:59.290 --> 00:52:03.510
and how the consequences of
that are accumulating over years

00:52:03.510 --> 00:52:05.880
to change our behavior.

00:52:05.880 --> 00:52:07.090
And I think that the real,

00:52:07.090 --> 00:52:08.396
what I'm really excited about the future

00:52:08.396 --> 00:52:11.523
of this area of research,

00:52:11.523 --> 00:52:16.047
is that there are a lot
of psychiatric disorders,

00:52:17.440 --> 00:52:20.810
the depression where people
are withdrawn socially,

00:52:20.810 --> 00:52:25.436
there's also autism or
schizophrenia and other areas,

00:52:25.436 --> 00:52:26.760
psychiatric disorders,

00:52:26.760 --> 00:52:31.276
where people are just resistant
to interacting socially

00:52:31.276 --> 00:52:33.130
or uncapable.

00:52:33.130 --> 00:52:35.770
That what we've been learning
through these animals

00:52:35.770 --> 00:52:37.250
and with this oxytocin system

00:52:37.250 --> 00:52:40.006
is how we might be able to
tweak the brain circuitry

00:52:40.006 --> 00:52:42.860
and tweak these neurochemicals

00:52:42.860 --> 00:52:47.820
so that we can help
improve the social outcome

00:52:47.820 --> 00:52:49.460
of these disorders.

00:52:49.460 --> 00:52:51.100
And that's really the reason

00:52:51.100 --> 00:52:54.050
why I'm doing the work that I'm doing

00:52:54.050 --> 00:52:59.050
is to try to to help us to
navigate the social world

00:52:59.440 --> 00:53:00.800
in a better way.

00:53:00.800 --> 00:53:02.410
- Yeah. Awesome.

00:53:02.410 --> 00:53:03.560
- That's so exciting.

00:53:03.560 --> 00:53:05.020
So encouraging in a lot of ways,

00:53:05.020 --> 00:53:05.853
especially as you mentioned,

00:53:05.853 --> 00:53:07.584
because it touches on so many different

00:53:07.584 --> 00:53:10.210
mental health challenges
and psychiatric illnesses.

00:53:10.210 --> 00:53:14.350
It really is key to how we
interact on a day-to-day basis.

00:53:14.350 --> 00:53:16.793
- Oh yeah, and so complicated.

00:53:20.040 --> 00:53:20.920
- I think in a lot of ways

00:53:20.920 --> 00:53:22.020
the listeners will really enjoy

00:53:22.020 --> 00:53:22.927
listening to this episode too.

00:53:22.927 --> 00:53:25.210
And just maybe even self evaluating,

00:53:25.210 --> 00:53:27.050
